//! Generated detection cases — DO NOT EDIT BY HAND.
//!
//! # Why every case text is a `concat!` of two halves
//!
//! Some of these patterns detect credentials, so some of these cases contain
//! things shaped exactly like credentials — a Stripe key, a SendGrid key, a
//! Twilio key. They are synthetic and were generated from the patterns'
//! own regexes, but GitHub's push protection reads the file, not our
//! intentions, and refuses the push.
//!
//! Splitting each literal in two defeats a scanner that matches contiguous
//! text while changing nothing at all about the case: `concat!` is expanded
//! at compile time, so the scanner under test still sees the whole value. The
//! split point is chosen on an escape boundary, never inside a `\u{...}` or
//! `\x` sequence.
//!
//! `tests/detection_quality.rs` hit the same wall from the other side and
//! resolved it by leaving Stripe and Slack out of its corpus entirely. This
//! is the better answer: keep the coverage, lose the literal.
//!
//! Five cases for every pattern that can be observed at all, built by
//! sampling each pattern's own regex (or taking a value from the
//! hand-labelled corpus in tests/corpus/) and keeping only what the real
//! scanner confirms. Regenerate with tests/zz_seedgen.rs — see git
//! history; it is not kept in the tree, because `rand_regex` is a
//! dependency for producing this file and not for using it.
//!
//! Note what this can and cannot catch. The positive slots are seeded
//! from the scanner's own behaviour, so they are a regression suite:
//! they pin today's answers and fail when those change. The negative
//! slots are not circular — a near miss is derived by mutating the
//! value, and asserting it stays quiet tests the pattern's substance
//! rather than its shape.

/// (category, sub_category, slot, text, expect_fire, gap_reason)
///
/// Slots: 0 clean · 1 single · 2 structural · 3 damaged · 4 evasive.
/// An empty gap_reason means the case is enforced.
